Inheritance is an OOP concept where a child class can reuse the variables and methods of a parent class.
Parent class: The class whose properties and methods are inherited.
Child class: The class that inherits from the parent class.

Single level inheritance:-
class A:
    def f1(self):
        print("f1 works")

    def f2(self):
        print("f2.works")

class B(A):
    def f3(self):
            print("f3 works")
    
    def f4(self):
            print("f4.works")


obj1 = B()
obj1.f1()


Multi level inheritance:-
class A:
    def f1(self):
        print("f1 works")

    def f2(self):
        print("f2.works")

class B(A): #inheritance
    def f3(self):
            print("f3 works")
    
    def f4(self):
            print("f4.works")

class C(B):
     def f5(self):
        print("f5 works")
     

obj1 = C()
obj1.f5()


Multiple inheritance:-

class A:
    def f1(self):
        print("f1 works")

    def f2(self):
        print("f2.works")

class B:
    def f3(self):
            print("f3 works")
    
    def f4(self):
            print("f4.works")

class C(A,B):
     def f5(self):
        print("f5 works")
     

obj1 = C()
obj1.f2()
obj1.f3()

MRO
------------
MRO stands for Method Resolution Order.
It determines the order in which Python searches for a method or attribute when a class uses inheritance.



class A:
    def f1(self):
        print("f1 works")

    def f2(self):
        print("f2.works")

    def show(self):
        print("from class A")

class B:
    def f3(self):
        print("f3 works")
    
    def f4(self):
        print("f4.works")

    def show(self):
        print("from class A")

class C(A,B):
     def f5(self):
        print("f5 works")
     

obj1 = C()
obj1.show()

Python will figure out the which class is first and then prints it if thier are same methods.

